WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.070
Model weights, visual components, context buffers, image tokens,

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:06.370
and runtime overhead all consume memory.

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:10.920
Available VRAM or unified memory often determines which model and quantization can run

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:12.100
without heavy offloading.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:16.019
System RAM still matters when weights or caches spill beyond accelerator memory.

00:00:16.019 --> 00:00:19.900
NVIDIA GPUs have broad local-inference support across many runtimes.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:23.995
AMD support depends more on operating system, backend, and model format.

00:00:23.995 --> 00:00:27.490
Apple Silicon uses unified memory shared by CPU and GPU,

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:30.640
which can make larger models practical but also means the operating system and

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:32.405
applications compete for the same pool.

00:00:32.405 --> 00:00:36.420
CPU inference is widely available but may be too slow for larger models or frequent

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:37.016
images.

00:00:37.016 --> 00:00:41.330
A GPU or accelerator improves throughput when the runtime supports it correctly.

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:42.800
Measure actual utilization;

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:46.615
unsupported operations can fall back to the CPU and change performance.

00:00:46.615 --> 00:00:50.000
Hardware tiers such as 4 to 8 gigabytes, 12 to 16 gigabytes,

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.000
and 24 gigabytes or more are useful planning categories,

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:54.800
not permanent model recommendations.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:57.800
Smaller tiers favor compact models and lower quantization.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:00.800
Larger tiers support more capacity, but still need headroom.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:04.100
Larger models can improve some tasks while increasing latency, power,

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:06.000
and failure risk under memory pressure.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.418
Concurrency and image size can matter more than a single prompt benchmark.

00:01:09.418 --> 00:01:13.400
Shortlist configurations that fit comfortably, then test speed, memory, temperature,

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.800
power, and completion under representative load.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:19.815
The right size is the smallest configuration that meets quality and reliability needs with

00:01:19.815 --> 00:01:21.000
operational headroom.
